## Migration: .NET Framework → .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()`** — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ (`SYSLIB0006`)
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()`** — unreliable, obsolete in .NET 9 (`SYSLIB0046`); only works if thread is in `WaitSleepJoin` state
3. **Race condition** — multiple `Enqueue` calls can signal `_newEvent` but `Reset()` + single `Dequeue()` loses items
4. **`ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()`** — not cancellation-aware

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while(true)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call interruption | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Timeout enforcement | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _processingTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard timeout

        if (_processingTask is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                await _processingTask;
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Expected on timeout — work was cancelled cooperatively
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### Key Changes

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` | `Channel<T>` + `Task.Run` | Thread-safe queue with built-in signaling; no manual lock |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` | Cooperative cancellation — the only option in .NET 6+ |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `_channel.Writer.Complete()` | Gracefully unblocks the reader |
| `lock` + `Queue<T>` | `Channel<T>` | Concurrent by design; eliminates the race condition |

### Common Pitfalls

1. **`ReadAllAsync` won't stop on `Cancel()` alone if an item is mid-`Handle()`** — if `Handle` is long-running, pass `token` into it and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints.
2. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks.
3. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — only catch it at the top-level orchestration point (like `StopAsync`).
4. **Graceful vs. hard stop** — `Complete()` lets in-flight items drain; `CancelAfter` is the hard deadline. Tune the timeout to your needs.

### If You Must Keep Raw Threads (Minimal Change)

```csharp
private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();

private void RunLoop()
{
    var token = _cts.Token;
    var handles = new[] { _newEvent, token.WaitHandle };

    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles); // wakes on event OR cancellation
        if (token.IsCancellationRequested) break;

        _newEvent.Reset();
        string item;
        lock (_lock) { item = _queue.Dequeue(); }
        Handle(item);
    }
}

public void Stop()
{
    _cts.Cancel();               // unblocks WaitAny immediately
    _thread.Join(5000);          // wait for graceful exit
    // No Thread.Abort — if it didn't stop, you have a bug to fix
    _cts.Dispose();
}
```

The `Channel<T>` approach is strongly recommended — it eliminates the manual synchronization entirely and is idiomatic .NET 8.
